it's great to welcome to the program  today Sanford Levinson who is a legal  scholar professor at the University of  Texas law school and co-author along  with his wife Cynthia of the book fault  lines in the Constitution the framers  their fights and the flaws that affect  us today a Sanford so great to talk to  you today I'm glad to be here so to  begin with for people who maybe aren't  familiar with the genesis of the  Constitution what was going on in the  United States that prompted the founding  fathers to realize that the Articles of  Confederation were in some way not  working or falling short and that  writing a constitution was something  that made sense  right there are three principal things  first of all the so called national  government I used that term advisedly  was incapable of getting any money what  they could do in effect was to ask the  states to contribute but by 1786 I think  they were contributing something like a  total of six hundred and thirty six  dollars so that's the first thing no  money secondly there were fears of  various military attacks from a variety  of European countries but also from a  variety of Indian tribes many of whom  for very sensible reasons had supported  the British during our secession from  the British Empire um and so there was  real concern about the ability of this  new country to defend itself the third  thing is that there were trade Wars  which is something we're really with  today in an international context but  there was no established notion of free  trade among the various states and there  was some genuine hostility between an  among a number of the states so that  people like Alexander Hamilton and  others described the system under the  artisan Federation is quote imbecilic  unquote and something had to be done  when the transition from the Articles of  Confederation to the Constitution to  place how much discussion do we know how  much discussion there was about writing  the Constitution in such a way to  indefinitely account for however society  might change and the reason I asked that  is that now there is a big debate over  to what degree do we need to be amending  the Constitution on an ongoing basis to  account for how drastically life has  changed today from when it was written  was there much discussion of accounting  for that at the time of its writing a  straight question I think that the most  accurate answer is no that the first  Constitution that is the article  Federation lasted a grand total of six  years I think that if you could have  interviewed the framers they would have  been thrilled to be told that the 1787  Constitution would last 50 years I think  if you said well you know you're really  writing something that should last  forever  I think they would have said you know  that's crazy in part because they were  extremely aware of what they repeatedly  called the lessons of experience and the  need to update government in terms of  those lessons Cynthia and I do not  engage and founder of ashing we think  that the founders by and large were  admirable people trying to respond to  what they thought were the chief  challenges of the day and that they  weren't utopias they were trying to  develop a document that could get us  through the next several years where  several might mean twenty-five or fifty  but they expected amendment and quite  frankly I think a lot of them expected  new conventions article 5 which is the  amendment article explicitly allows for  new conventions and I think that most of  the framers would be astonished to be  told  there's been no national convention  since 1787 where has there been over 230  state constitutional conventions since  1787 so there is the view from some that  it is immensely difficult to amend the  Constitution so my question is sort of a  two-part question the first part is how  do you assess the difficulty of amending  the Constitution in the context of sort  of modern society and number two if  indeed the founders were so welcoming or  expecting of significant amending of the  Constitution as the country changed why  did they set the level of difficulty  that they set right another great  question what they faced in something  eighty-seven was an article  Confederation that required unanimous  consent of the state legislatures of  every state so from their perspective  article 5 which requires quote only  unquote three quarters of the states to  assent to amendment was a big  improvement secondly article 5 worked in  the first let's call it 15 or 20 years  we got the Bill of Rights in 1791 our 12  amendments were sent out ten of them  were ratified we call them today the  Bill of Rights in 1800 or 1801 the  problems to put it mildly of the way we  selected presidents was revealed in the  tie between Thomas Jefferson and Aaron  Burr and we got the twelfth amendment  which changed things for the better  though not enough as far as I'm  concerned and I'm skipping the 11th  amendment which is much more esoteric  but in 1793 the Supreme Court issued a  very controversial decision about states  rights and it was reversed so if you  look at our  early history you could say we know 3/4  is a lot but the system is more or less  working in the modern world 3/4 of 50  where you have the distribution that you  now have of small states and indeed very  small states and not only large states  but very large states and a far far more  polarized country then even was the case  in 1787 it has become functionally close  to impossible to amend the Constitution  so you know political scientists have  looked at that looked at this and some  have said United States National  Constitution is the most difficult to  amend Constitution of the world and one  of the things we talk about in our book  and frankly I think one of the things  that is especially valuable about our  book is that we look not only at the US  Constitution but also at the fact that  every state has its own Constitution  there are 51 constitutions the United  States and if you look at the state  constitutions they are all easier to  amend than the National Constitution so  it's not the case that the American  people are reluctant to amend their  constitutions in fact the typical state  has had close to three different  constitutions over their histories  rather we have a peculiar religious like  veneration of the National Constitution  that makes it almost unthinkable to talk  about amending it but even if you do  think of amending you then come up  against the concrete reality that it's  extraordinary difficult to get  two-thirds of each house of Congress and  then even if you get it it's even more  difficult to get three-quarters of the  states so one of the areas that is of a  lot of interest right now because of the  current administration is the Census and  how should it be carried out what is  really the underlying purpose of the  census from from a sort of  constitutional historian perspective  what was the point of establishing the  census to begin with since is an  extraordinarily interesting part of the  Constitution because you can make an  argument that it's really one of the  very very few things Congress must make  sure is done the other is actually  setting up a Supreme Court of the United  States but for example setting up  circuit courts and district courts  that's optional with Congress it's  optional for Congress to declare war and  not to declare wars etc etc but the  Constitution requires that a census be  held every ten years and the reason is  deceptively simple it's the house  representatives that the house is  allocated more or less proportionately  unlike the US Senate and so if you're  going to have a major branch of Congress  where representation is a function of  the population of the States you have to  know the population of the states it's  really as simple as that  and it's every 10 years because the  framers were smart enough to realize  that the United States was that I name a  country there would be lots of  settlement there would be people moving  around so you couldn't necessarily  depend that the population of Virginia  in 1790 relative to the population of  Tennessee would be the same in 1800 or  1820 etc etc so the Constitution  requires every 10 years without  exception interestingly in my reading  although it requires it it's not clear  what the penalty would be and to whom it  would be assessed if the census does not  happen as scheduled is my reading  correct of that well you know it's one  of those things John Marshall refer to  the Constitution as  great outline and so there are things  that are specified one of them is you  gotta have a census but again the  question you ask is is a very shrewd one  what if the president says you know I  don't think so  then what do you do do you charge the  president with violating the law well  actually this morning is being spent on  the mower hearings and hearing Robert  mower saying over and over again the  Justice Department says you can't indict  it's sitting president  so could you impeach a president for  refusing to take care to enforce one of  the most basic obligations of the  national government which is to conduct  a census I think the answer is yes but I  think it would require a sense of  national outrage led by Congress that  the president is being derelict with  regard to one of his most fundamental  responsibilities which is to make sure  the house representatives is fairly  apportioned yes and in today's hyper  partisan climate it's hard for me to  imagine that there would be that level  of United Miss around that particular  issue but it remains to be seen right I  think you're right we've been speaking  with Sanford Levinson who's a legal  scholar professor at the University of  Texas law school the book co-authored  with his wife Cynthia is fault lines in  the Constitution the framers their  fights and the flaws that affect us  today  Sanford really a pleasure having you on  today my pleasure

